## Package comparison

The round-1 phase package remains aligned with approved Phase 1. The rework stays within that package’s file scope.

## Findings

### Critical — The Effect-v4 runtime architecture remains unimplemented

The coordinator, provider, server, and client are still Promise/class code using mutable arrays, callbacks, and raw `setTimeout`. There are still no Effect services/layers, scoped fibers/streams, `SubscriptionRef`, bounded Effect `Queue`, `Schedule`, or `TestClock`. Importing `effect/Schema` in `protocol.ts` does not satisfy the phase’s defining requirement that runtime ownership and resource lifecycles be Effect-scoped.

### High — Schema ingress is still only partial and continues casting parsed JSON

The rework added schemas, but their decoded values are often discarded in favor of manual reconstruction and casts (`protocol.ts:231-287`). State events do not use `StateEventSchema`; `player()` manually casts at `protocol.ts:212` and treats a missing/non-object `track` or `device` the same as `null`, so malformed player states can still reach client caches. Hello response `data` remains `unknown` and is partially cast in `client.ts:329`, with no schema validation of daemon protocol/package/capability fields. This still violates “decode unknown values at both ingress points; do not cast parsed JSON.”

### High — Shutdown settles an active caller but does not interrupt its work

`coordinator.dispose()` marks and rejects `#activeJob`, but the underlying `await this.#provider.transport(...)` at `coordinator.ts:209` continues. If it later succeeds, execution still calls `accept()`—which does not reject closed coordinators—and creates a new reconciliation timer after the existing timer set was cleared. Thus state/provider work can continue after disposal even though the caller was settled. An owning Effect scope must interrupt the active command and prevent all post-close projection/scheduling.

### High — Required acceptance coverage is still absent

The session suite remains five tests across four files. It still lacks evidence for replayed status/state caches, malformed/oversized-client isolation, stale sample rejection, coalesced sampling, 3/5/8 scheduling, command failure continuation, queue overflow, optimistic seek, reconciliation timing, out-of-order correlation, incompatible hello, disconnect/indeterminate commands, listener isolation, and exact-once shutdown. None of the reworked schema or lifecycle cases received regression tests. The coder result also substitutes `npm pack ./packages/music-core --dry-run` for the package’s exact command, which remains unverified as written.

### Medium — Framing corrupts UTF-8 split across chunks

`framing.ts:20` creates a new `TextDecoder` for every `push(..., { stream: true })`, so decoder state is not retained when a multibyte character is split between socket chunks. This violates incremental UTF-8 framing and is not covered by the protocol tests. Retain one decoder per framer and test split multibyte input.
